WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.510
Dave Rawlings: I hate how
he's holding his sword.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.060
Orlando Bloom is holding his sword

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.780
in a manner that upsets me.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.020
Hi, there. Dave Rawlings here

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.640
at the London Longsword Academy.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.350
And you may recall, I
did a video looking at,

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:17.600
how realistic are sword-fight
scenes within films?

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.470
We're gonna look at a few
different fight scenes

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.460
and see what we think is realistic

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.410
or what is good in a sword-fight scene.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.436
This fight scene has to be seen

00:00:34.436 --> 00:00:36.087
in the context of "f--- it,"

00:00:36.087 --> 00:00:38.237
because if you've got that many opponents,

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:39.070
what can you do?

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.000
There is something which I would suggest

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.920
is a little bit similar
to some of the actions

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.537
in one of the treaties I studied.

00:00:44.537 --> 00:00:46.875
Which is, basically, run away a lot,

00:00:46.875 --> 00:00:49.875
and then when somebody
gets close enough to you,

00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:52.541
cut them, spin and cover
your head as you go,

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:53.741
and then run away.

00:00:53.741 --> 00:00:56.286
That wouldn't be such a
good look for Aragorn.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.110
So, I'm pretty sure he could find

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.250
a more efficient way of doing this.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.180
But that said, I think there's a moment

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:11.060
of ultimate sacrifice in
which he gets rescued.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.530
So I think there's really
this feeling, like I say,

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.620
it's just, "I'm going to save these people

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.820
no matter what. If I
die, it doesn't matter."

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.683
So, this is the eponymous longsword.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.510
The longsword. And, as you can see,

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.837
this particular example
is not the thin thing

00:01:28.837 --> 00:01:30.980
that you see in a lot
of HEMA competitions.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.180
This is designed to
cleave things quite well

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.623
but also to thrust them.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.210
I never mind seeing cuts to legs.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:47.090
You have lots and lots of
cuts, and that's great.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.270
There's parries. That's great.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.080
But there's not this
awareness of space around you.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.070
And the biggest use of anything

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:58.136
is that maintenance of
this safe space around him,

00:01:58.136 --> 00:01:59.936
not letting people get close.

00:01:59.936 --> 00:02:02.980
This is where the study of
montante is really, really good.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.320
I do not care which of you I hurt,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.140
so I'm making the space
as big as possible.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.930
And that, I feel, is unused
in this a little bit.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.523
So, I think probably a six, maybe.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.084
If you've seen the

00:02:22.084 --> 00:02:24.984
Obi-Wan, Qui-Gon Jinn, Darth Maul fight

00:02:24.984 --> 00:02:28.075
in the prequels, this is beautiful.

00:02:28.075 --> 00:02:29.930
You have Obi-Wan's signature move,

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.640
and then you have this
change into Qui-Gon.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.930
This is the key.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.510
This is something that sums up

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:39.560
all martial arts for
me, which is evolution.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.435
Learning, studying your sources,

00:02:42.435 --> 00:02:44.700
learning everything that
you've made mistakes

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.840
by allowing yourself to make mistakes.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.550
Everything in fencing where
we are not dying is education.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.940
And this is perfect,
because you have Darth Maul

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.870
doing the same thing that
he's done in the prequels.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.345
And Obi-Wan, expecting this
same smash in the face,

00:03:01.345 --> 00:03:02.507
rather than trying to parry

00:03:02.507 --> 00:03:04.240
severs the blade with the simplest answer.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.006
It's a Gordian-knot moment.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.630
When you look at
double-bladed lightsabers,

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.710
I don't want you to look for something

00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:16.000
which is going to be
the same within swords.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.550
Look at polearms. So, that could be staff,

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:19.900
it could be halberd.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.800
It's something where both ends

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.920
have the potential to do damage.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.200
So, this would be what
I would call half-staff,

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.700
because you're holding it
more or less in the middle.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.050
And obviously that
structure is dictated a bit

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.690
by how wide apart you can hold your hands,

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.570
so it changes the fighting
style a little bit.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:36.292
And then with that,
obviously you can thrust,

00:03:36.292 --> 00:03:38.102
because your reach with your forward hand

00:03:38.102 --> 00:03:39.800
is still going to be the
same, whatever you're doing.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:40.840
So you can thrust.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.280
You can stab this way.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.365
There's a lot of potential
with a weapon like this.

00:03:44.365 --> 00:03:45.620
It's 10. It's 10.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.453
It's beautiful.

00:03:46.453 --> 00:03:47.853
If you're allowed 15, it's 15.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.340
As far as swordplay is concerned,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.922
this to me looks very, very

00:03:54.922 --> 00:03:57.018
just like a classic saber.

00:03:57.018 --> 00:03:59.635
It's not posh. It's got parry riposte.

00:03:59.635 --> 00:04:00.468
That's what you need.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.440
When you get close, ideally
you don't just want to

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.890
cut from side to side on the sword.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:10.720
It's not a good use of your time.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.790
We, in general, would prefer,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.723
in most of the systems that I study,

00:04:15.723 --> 00:04:16.848
to stay on the blade

00:04:16.848 --> 00:04:19.370
and then to thrust the
other side of the hilt.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.940
And that will encourage your opponent

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.240
either to have to block,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.989
or if they don't have to block,

00:04:23.989 --> 00:04:25.360
then they'll leave themselves open.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:26.770
Just beating down the sword.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.560
Perfectly fine, good thrust,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.450
and moving out of the way of the thrust,

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:32.070
such a lovely thing.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.460
A combination of cover and
getting out of the way.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.310
Perfect. No problem with that at all.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.250
If I have one complaint,

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.770
it's that whenever anybody gets close,

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:46.980
you have this arm that is floating around,

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:47.900
waiting for the point

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.460
that it does something with the dagger.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.570
It'd be really nice when
there's these closures,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:52.950
smack someone in the face,

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.163
push them on the elbow to turn them away.

00:05:01.532 --> 00:05:02.780
The finish is beautiful.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.000
The finish is absolutely lovely.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.322
The fact that he gives a blow,

00:05:07.322 --> 00:05:08.873
he still, even at the end,

00:05:08.873 --> 00:05:11.303
maintains distance, keeps his distance,

00:05:11.303 --> 00:05:13.610
delivers something to get
the opponent to react.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.763
There's a thrust, then a
recovery to see what happens.

00:05:16.763 --> 00:05:18.300
That is lovely.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.005
Just the stab, get out of the way.

00:05:20.005 --> 00:05:21.910
"Are you all right, mate?
Oh, no, you're dead."

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.180
There are three things that you want to do

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.110
if you want to make
sure that your opponent

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.320
is not going to still try
and mess you up afterwards.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.120
No. 1, as you see in
this, is stab and get out.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.230
No. 2 is something that
Fabris and Thibault use,

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:40.740
which is that you smash
the hilts together,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.630
so the opponent's blade is
effectively squashed into them

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.430
or out of the line of attack,

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.020
and then you get out afterwards.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:49.800
Third is to carry out some
kind of movement of conclusion.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.620
And you see this in different systems,

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:53.650
where you grab the opponent's blade

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.680
with your left hand or
your secondary hand,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.940
you immobilize it, then you give them

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.727
a bit of slice and dice,
and then you either

00:06:00.727 --> 00:06:03.700
take their sword away or
you keep control of it.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.400
I think this is, honestly, this is a 10.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.210
The fact that there is clearly, clearly

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.203
a definite fencing system going in there.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:16.390
Right at the beginning,

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:18.070
where there's this
sliding along the blade,

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.950
from a Destreza point
of view, this is lovely.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.490
The measure is terrible.

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:22.970
Someone should have
been stabbed in the arm,

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:24.930
but then they're not really
trying to kill each other.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.402
You do it occasionally

00:06:26.402 --> 00:06:28.710
just to see what the
other person is doing.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.830
Can I get control of your
blade to do something?

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.015
It's not well illustrated,

00:06:32.015 --> 00:06:34.075
but if you were being nice about it,

00:06:34.075 --> 00:06:36.254
you could say it's Verdadera
Destreza. It's not.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.230
I hate how he's holding his sword.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.800
Orlando Bloom is holding his sword

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.550
in a manner that upsets me.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:47.830
Put your thumb on the flat of the blade.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.510
You're going to have much more ability

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:50.600
to hold on to your sword.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.790
If you wanted to turn your
hand over slightly, then do so.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:54.820
But this just kind of feels

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:56.973
like a hideous way to lose your sword.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:02.680
This is all fine.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.652
It's very, very slow.

00:07:03.652 --> 00:07:04.843
It's just plodding through.

00:07:04.843 --> 00:07:06.570
Distance is maintained.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.130
So, when we're talking about
distance in fight scenes,

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:12.030
really what we're looking
at is a perfect distance

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:15.258
whereby we can disadvantage our opponent.

00:07:15.258 --> 00:07:17.430
So the ideal situation is for us to be

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.780
at a distance where we
can just hit our opponent,

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:20.737
putting him with our hand,

00:07:20.737 --> 00:07:21.978
or maybe by putting him with our hand

00:07:21.978 --> 00:07:23.553
and a little bit of a lean of our body,

00:07:23.553 --> 00:07:25.580
because that means that
we're free to move.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.202
What we want from our opponent

00:07:27.202 --> 00:07:30.992
is a minimum of needing
to reach us with a step.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.610
The sword in the door is a
very, very interesting thing,

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.043
because what you have is
something which tapers

00:07:40.043 --> 00:07:41.826
to a very, very small point

00:07:41.826 --> 00:07:44.401
with a fricking great
lever on the end of it,

00:07:44.401 --> 00:07:47.395
that, if you went waggle
waggle on the lever,

00:07:47.395 --> 00:07:48.386
would intrinsically --

00:07:48.386 --> 00:07:49.494
all of the functions of a sword

00:07:49.494 --> 00:07:52.260
that make it good to
actually stick into something

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.980
also kind of make it
relatively easy to remove.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.620
So if you've just got the point in there

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.803
and you go up and down a couple of times,

00:07:58.803 --> 00:07:59.744
that's coming out.

00:07:59.744 --> 00:08:01.772
This is getting a six.

00:08:01.772 --> 00:08:04.813
There's bits in there
that are passable fencing.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:13.403
One of the things I never
understand about lightsabers

00:08:13.403 --> 00:08:14.660
is they're always portrayed

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.980
as being these slow, cumbersome things.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:18.240
And I don't think it's intentional,

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.940
but you have techniques here where,

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.550
where you have this
cross against the blade,

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:22.730
the simplest thing would just to be

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.250
to cut around so quickly.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.740
With a longsword you would
do this insanely fast.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.400
It's such a fast, sharp maneuver,

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.488
and there's no reason to think

00:08:30.488 --> 00:08:31.565
that this would be any different.

00:08:31.565 --> 00:08:33.488
So I don't know why you have this

00:08:33.488 --> 00:08:35.430
long, slow press against the wall.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:36.690
There's so many things you could do.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:38.390
You could wind in from the other side.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.890
You could cut to the other side.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.400
So, spear and staff are
intrinsically interlinked,

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:44.575
so there's really not a problem.

00:08:44.575 --> 00:08:45.963
These single-handed releases,

00:08:45.963 --> 00:08:47.680
where you whirl with the
back end of the staff,

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.520
totally a thing. Totally effective.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.498
You can see the only thing
really that's going on in there

00:08:53.498 --> 00:08:55.610
is that as he's delivering
this downward blow,

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.018
he's really trying

00:08:57.018 --> 00:08:58.792
not to smash the other person in the head,

00:08:58.792 --> 00:09:00.320
which is kind of courteous.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.940
So it's not being delivered with the force

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.240
that you might realistically see.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:06.270
Kicking this with the foot,

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.760
this is something you see in montante

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.220
in order to start the generation.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.908
The thing that I think is possibly missing

00:09:10.908 --> 00:09:12.507
from this sight is, really,

00:09:12.507 --> 00:09:14.880
there's two advantages
that the Mandalorian has.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.440
First of all, the
Darksaber is not any good

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:18.800
at cutting through his armor,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.010
so he can pretty much do what
he likes most of the time.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.151
And, secondly, he's not using

00:09:22.151 --> 00:09:23.759
the full advantage of his spear.

00:09:23.759 --> 00:09:25.770
Usually when you're seeing
a spear against a sword,

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:27.650
what you're looking at is
somebody holding a spear

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:28.960
really on the last quarter

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.400
and trying to maintain
distance as much as possible

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.810
and really letting the
point jab as quickly,

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.380
and as quickly as possible.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.127
But, yeah, I think entertaining,

00:09:37.127 --> 00:09:39.420
and does contain a lot of
realistic technique in it.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.993
I think I would give this
a six, seven out of 10.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.900
I absolutely love "Rob Roy."

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:53.290
There are little bits of problems.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:55.792
First one is that there's not very much

00:09:55.792 --> 00:09:58.000
point work from Liam Neeson.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.690
The idea that the broadsword cannot thrust

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:01.523
is completely wrong.

00:10:01.523 --> 00:10:03.410
Zachary Wylde, who does a small-sword

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.940
and a broadsword treaty,
says that the broadsword

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:07.800
has the advantage over the small sword

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.487
because it can both thrust and cut.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.150
Tim Roth is a much better swordsman.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.060
And this is, again, set
up through the whole film.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.770
He knows he's the villain of the piece.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:27.860
He sets himself up as
the villain of the piece.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:30.600
So don't look at Liam
Neeson's character, Rob Roy,

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:32.580
as being a good fighter in this.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.512
He's somebody who can hack and slash.

00:10:34.512 --> 00:10:37.360
He isn't aware fully of the full capacity

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.890
of the weapon that he's using,

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:41.793
and Tim Roth is just
massively outclassing him.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.500
I love that there's good
distance in this film.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.185
People not just hanging around in distance

00:10:51.185 --> 00:10:54.420
messing around with each
other's perception of space.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:55.892
Not just a linear fight.

00:10:55.892 --> 00:10:57.980
Circular parries, following the sword.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:59.610
Wonderful things. Wonderful. Good.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.370
I've seen this duck under
the arm criticized a lot.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.980
This is completely viable.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:06.800
If somebody is coming down
with quite a diagonal movement,

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.110
maybe not so much if they're doing

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.850
a vertical movement, you
can get away with it.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:11.650
If they're coming through
a diagonal movement,

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:14.170
they come in by necessity
higher than your head

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:15.910
in order to cleave through your head.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:17.544
This leaves you a big triangle of space

00:11:17.544 --> 00:11:18.520
that you can step into.

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:19.810
Usually you'll put the sword

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:21.842
in the way to keep yourself safe,

00:11:21.842 --> 00:11:23.840
but that awareness of that triangle

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:25.863
that you can step into, very much there.

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:35.500
And this is the perfect hubris death.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.150
It's absolutely wonderful.

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.290
People get so worried
about grabbing blades,

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.070
but if it's the difference
between you dying

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:43.503
and the other person,

00:11:43.503 --> 00:11:45.011
absolutely, it's what you'll do.

00:11:45.011 --> 00:11:47.110
You see this throughout so many manuals,

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:48.250
and every time you show a picture of it,

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.887
some wisea-- on the internet goes,

00:11:49.887 --> 00:11:50.893
"Oh, you'd never grab a sword!"

00:11:50.893 --> 00:11:52.757
Yes, you would, if you don't want to die.

00:11:52.757 --> 00:11:55.033
Ratings-wise, I'm giving this a 10.

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:01.830
First of all, within this fight scene,

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.250
we have something thrown
in a simple deflection.

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:05.850
Just roll it and carry on.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.802
So you're not trying to
obscure your vision very long,

00:12:07.802 --> 00:12:09.052
and then you're piling in.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.294
You could say there's a problem

00:12:12.294 --> 00:12:14.460
in that he then immediately
goes back to attack

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.800
at the side which has got
the most protection on it,

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:19.370
which is not necessarily the best use

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:20.810
when going against a large shield.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.533
You don't want to keep on
cracking at the big object.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:28.510
This rotation and cutting
around, absolutely lovely.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:30.350
Now, there's different
ways you can do this,

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.000
but that is a very good just
rotating with the weapon

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.190
and cutting through on this side.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:37.260
If you have contact and
you can spin inside that,

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:38.942
it's very, very much a thing to do.

00:12:38.942 --> 00:12:41.410
It makes it very hard for
your opponent to defend.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.130
You have to be cautious.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:45.311
It's overly used towards the end of this,

00:12:45.311 --> 00:12:47.213
and the fight scene
starts getting fatigued.

00:12:53.246 --> 00:12:55.680
It's kind of gone into old Viking tropes

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.320
where everybody has to hit the
weapons really, really hard.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.920
And this does an incredible disservice

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.890
to the lightness and the maneuverability

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.910
of some of these weapons.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:07.451
A lot of Viking swords are so nippy

00:13:07.451 --> 00:13:08.570
when you handle the originals.

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:09.403
Not all of them.

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.930
Women have been fencing
since the very beginning

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.040
of recorded swordsmanship manuals.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:18.290
Our very, very first fencing manual

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:19.660
is the very first fencing manual

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.140
with a woman fencer in it.

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:22.220
That's all you need to know.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.861
Now, in this fight scene,

00:13:24.861 --> 00:13:27.335
you have somebody who is technically

00:13:27.335 --> 00:13:29.397
using their weaponry
better than the other.

00:13:29.397 --> 00:13:31.770
The dialogue is between two fencers,

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:33.770
one of which is better. It's not gendered,

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.930
and this is a really, really cool thing.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:38.940
So, I think probably a six, maybe.

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:41.230
Maybe a five, six. It
starts off very well,

00:13:41.230 --> 00:13:42.990
and then it deteriorates into trope,

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.232
which is a bit of a shame.

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:52.750
Oh, this is always interesting.

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.155
Whenever you have blades which are in

00:13:55.155 --> 00:13:56.880
what I call a passive position,

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:59.040
this makes it incredibly difficult

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.190
for you to gain dominance over them.

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:02.620
And this is their strength.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:04.850
If you have two angles like this,

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:07.701
it's quite easy for you to try and wrest

00:14:07.701 --> 00:14:09.043
for a little bit of dominance

00:14:09.043 --> 00:14:10.976
and try and feel very, very strongly

00:14:10.976 --> 00:14:12.020
what your opponent's doing

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.870
by putting pressure on their blade.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.550
When somebody is in this
passive and this passive,

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:19.460
it can be incredibly
dangerous for you to do so

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:21.650
because they can disengage so quickly.

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:23.787
This is a beautiful start to the fight.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:30.080
No crossing the distance.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:31.870
There's just this wait,

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:34.086
waiting to see when it's the right time

00:14:34.086 --> 00:14:35.652
for you to go forward.

00:14:35.652 --> 00:14:38.260
This, this, this, even this, is just,

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:39.720
this is good. This is nice.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:41.620
If your distance management is thus that

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:44.170
as they keep on coming
forward, they can't reach you.

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.453
So he's encouraging
his opponent to commit.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:53.733
And then as his opponent goes to go around

00:14:53.733 --> 00:14:55.350
to go above this bit of hilt,

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:57.192
it's only enough, but it's enough for him

00:14:57.192 --> 00:14:59.468
to reposition himself beneath the hilt,

00:14:59.468 --> 00:15:02.690
and that, to rotate the
palm down, a simple thrust.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:04.240
This is a beautiful fight scene.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:06.032
Obviously, it's "The
Duellists"! It's a 10.

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:13.270
I hate this. I hate this so much.

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:14.628
So, again, in general,

00:15:14.628 --> 00:15:16.652
the majority of this fight is too close,

00:15:16.652 --> 00:15:18.710
and it kind of gets better as it goes on.

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:20.570
The distance becomes more elongated.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:23.274
So I don't know if they shot
this the wrong way around,

00:15:23.274 --> 00:15:24.850
or whatever's happening.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:28.150
Anything where you do not
have protection in your hands,

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:29.833
if you want to parry something,

00:15:29.833 --> 00:15:31.108
stab at the other thing.

00:15:31.108 --> 00:15:34.250
If they go up, stab at their
hands. Attack the hands.

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.050
There is not this intrinsic awareness of,

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.505
"That's the thing coming
forwards. I can injure that."

00:15:39.505 --> 00:15:42.110
I'm not sure where these
daggers are supposed to be from,

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:43.768
but I would not suggest

00:15:43.768 --> 00:15:46.217
that they're really meant to be Egyptian.

00:15:46.217 --> 00:15:48.240
They look an awful lot like sai.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.010
Maybe there's some
cross-pollination. Who knows?

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:51.720
It's not a particularly good fight,

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:53.001
so I would give this a four.

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:01.360
I really don't know how
to rate this fight scene,

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.550
because there is no clarity to it.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:04.930
There is no reason for the guy,

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.280
as far as I can see,
to fall off the horse.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:09.260
There is an awful lot of sword waving

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:11.740
that doesn't seem to be
particularly applicable.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:13.230
You have someone who, earlier in the film,

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.208
is supposed to be good at
swordplay and to understand it

00:16:15.208 --> 00:16:16.858
who suddenly thinks just
waving your arm around

00:16:16.858 --> 00:16:19.420
is a really good thing to do,

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:20.920
doesn't have a good sense of timing

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:21.953
of how to hit somebody,

00:16:21.953 --> 00:16:23.286
supposed to be experienced.

00:16:23.286 --> 00:16:24.550
Within the disciplines I study,

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:25.955
there is an art called Roßfechten,

00:16:25.955 --> 00:16:28.080
which basically means
fighting on horseback.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.250
Now, within that, there are ways

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:31.460
of controlling somebody's
balance on the horse,

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:34.100
making them lose control
of their horse's balance

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:36.280
for example, or just the
movement of the horse.

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:37.520
So you might slice their bridle.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:38.530
You might cut the bridle.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.230
You may control the bridle yourself

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.470
to try and rotate the horse's neck.

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:45.420
Those things are very, very
well known within the community.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:47.178
You also have techniques on foot,

00:16:47.178 --> 00:16:49.411
which are things like, with a spear,

00:16:49.411 --> 00:16:51.530
trying to trip the horse
and break its legs.

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:53.430
None of that's in this.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:55.660
This just appears to be
something that happened.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:57.580
There was some arm waving, and
then a man fell off a horse.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:16:59.210
Nobody really knows what's going on.

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.960
So this is getting a two,
because I don't understand it.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:03.840
This, I have really no idea

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:05.840
what's supposed to have happened at all.

